{
  "name": "Chart dots layer",
  "description": "Reading dots as `chartDots` layers. Every dots layer draws in its chart's frame, not its own, so each resolves to the chart's frame. `indices` lists the readings that get a dot (holes and the chart's own highlight dots left out), empty when the layer draws nothing; `diameter` is the effective dot size in design points (`size`, else the chart's lineWidth times 1.8), 0 when the chart is missing; `size` and `colorHex` are absent when the layer stores none. Chart A (line 1,6,2,4, half the width) carries three dots layers: one on every reading at size 5 in orange, one automatic at the automatic 3.6 (29.5 points apart, room for 10.8), and a hidden one at size 12. The largest visible drawing layer is 5, so the chart's stroke inset grows to 2.5: a line anchored `highest`, `through` stands at x 31 of the 90.5-point plot (30.5 without the dots) and runs from 2.5 to 63 down the 65.5-point box. The hidden layer does not widen it. Chart B has ten readings and an automatic layer at size 4, 9.83 points apart where 12 are needed, so it draws none. Chart C is bars, which never carry dots. Chart E highlights both ends, so its all-dots layer skips readings 1 and 2, where the chart draws its own dots. The last layer links to a chart the document does not have and keeps its own frame.",
